Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: Recover from spurious Rosetta faults on W|X and X pages.

Under Rosetta 2 a write to a page that is both writable and executable, or
an execution from an executable page, can fault although the protection
allows it (Rosetta keeps stale state for pages whose code it translated).
Wine turned that into an access violation for the program. Toggle the exec
bit to make Rosetta drop its state and resume.

Ported from CrossOver 26.3 sources (CW Hack 24945, 25719).
(c) CodeWeavers, Inc.; LGPL-2.1-or-later.
---
diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
index b61da59..7049ef9 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -4287,6 +4287,23 @@ NTSTATUS virtual_handle_fault( struct thread_data *data, EXCEPTION_RECORD *rec,
         WARN( "treating read fault in a readable page as a write fault, addr %p\n", addr );
         err = EXCEPTION_WRITE_FAULT;
     }
+
+    /* Rosetta can raise spurious write faults on pages that are writable and
+     * executable, and exec faults on executable pages (e.g. after code on them
+     * was translated). Toggling exec makes Rosetta drop its stale state. */
+    if ((err == EXCEPTION_WRITE_FAULT &&
+         (get_unix_prot( vprot ) & (PROT_WRITE | PROT_EXEC)) == (PROT_WRITE | PROT_EXEC)) ||
+        (err == EXCEPTION_EXECUTE_FAULT && (get_unix_prot( vprot ) & PROT_EXEC)))
+    {
+        FIXME( "spurious %s fault on a %s page, addr %p\n",
+               err == EXCEPTION_WRITE_FAULT ? "write" : "exec",
+               err == EXCEPTION_WRITE_FAULT ? "w|x" : "x", addr );
+        mprotect_range( page, host_page_size, 0, VPROT_EXEC );
+        mprotect_range( page, host_page_size, VPROT_EXEC, 0 );
+        mutex_unlock( &virtual_mutex );
+        rec->ExceptionCode = STATUS_SUCCESS;
+        return STATUS_SUCCESS;
+    }
 #endif
 
     if (!is_inside_signal_stack( data, stack ) && (vprot & VPROT_GUARD))
